    "body": "## Problem\n\nCloses #30647.\n\n`:profile` only writes its output file when `:profile stop` or `:profile dump` runs. If Nvim\nhangs in a way that can't be interrupted, neither command can be issued, so no profile data is\nleft behind at all — exactly the case the issue asks about (\"in case the hang was infinite ...\nthere is really no way to debug\").\n\n## Solution\n\nWrite the profile file periodically from the existing per-line profiling hooks\n(`script_line_end()`, `func_line_end()`), throttled to at most once per second. Those hooks run\nsynchronously on every executed script and function line, so the file on disk keeps getting\nrefreshed while Nvim runs, and survives a hard kill.\n\n`profile_dump()` already reopens with `\"w\"`, so each write replaces the previous contents;\nnothing accumulates.\n\n+43 lines: `src/nvim/profile.c` (+35), `runtime/doc/repeat.txt` (+8).\n\n## Testing\n\nBuilt on Windows x64 / MSVC 19.44, RelWithDebInfo, and A/B'd against unpatched master in the\nsame tree with only `profile.c` differing.\n\nTest: `:profile start` + `:profile func Spin`, then an uninterruptible `while` loop; the process\nis then killed with no `:profile stop` and no `:profile dump`.\n\n| | this branch | unpatched master |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| profile file exists while hung | yes, within ~1s | no |\n| file present after hard kill | yes, with data | no file at all |\n| refreshed while still hung | yes (mtime advances) | n/a |\n\nRecovered from the killed process:\n\n```\ncount  total (s)   self (s)\n2999903              1.472708   while i >= 0\n2999902              1.886278     let i += 1\n2999902              1.927935     let j = i * 2\n```\n\n## Trade-offs and open questions\n\nFlagging these up front rather than waiting for review:\n\n1. **It costs ~3.4% on profiled code.** A 3M-iteration profiled loop went 6.69s -> 6.92s (3 runs\n   each). That is not the I/O — only ~6 writes of a 655-byte file. It is the `os_hrtime()` that\n   `profile_may_dump()` performs on *every* profiled line to check the throttle. It could be\n   made nearly free by gating the clock read behind a line counter (e.g. consult the clock every\n   Nth line). Happy to do that if you'd like it in this PR.\n2. **Unconditional vs. an option.** In\n   [#30647 (comment)](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/issues/30647#issuecomment-2392024251)\n   @justinmk suggested *\"a 'streaming' option for `:profile start`\"*. This PR instead makes the\n   behavior unconditional, which imposes (1) on everyone who profiles. If you'd prefer\n   `:profile start {fname} streaming` or similar, say so and I'll rework it — that seems likely\n   to be the better shape given the cost.\n3. **The write is not atomic.** `profile_dump()` truncates in place, so a kill landing during a\n   write leaves a partial file — the very scenario this targets. A temp-file-plus-rename would\n   fix it; not done here to keep the diff small.\n4. **Vimscript only.** Updates happen only from the Vimscript line hooks, so a hang inside C or\n   Lua produces no further writes. The doc change says so explicitly. This does not address the\n   \"`:profile` should also show Lua function calls\" part of the issue.\n5. **No automated test yet.** A functional test (start profiling, run lines, wait past the\n   interval, assert the file is non-empty *before* `:profile stop`) is the obvious addition; I\n   held off pending the outcome of (2), since an opt-in flag changes what the test looks like.\n\nAlso note the branch is based on `a7f422fa48` and was not re-tested against the newest master\n(~117 commits newer at the time of opening); `profile.c` is the only file it touches.\n\n## Provenance\n\nThe implementation and the testing described above were done with AI assistance (Claude); the\ncommit carries a `Co-Authored-By` trailer. A human has reviewed the diff and the measurements\nbefore submitting. Opened as a draft because of the open design question in (2).\n",
